# Cloudflare Pages reads this from the deployed root, like `_headers`.
#
# `expo export` writes one HTML file per route and flattens the group segments,
# so `/discover` is `discover.html` and Pages serves it as an asset. A *dynamic*
# route has no such file: `app/[username].tsx` exports `[username].html`, which
# Pages will only ever serve at the literal path `/[username]`. Without the rule
# below, `app2.langx.io/behic` is a 404 — which is the entire feature.
#
# One segment, not `/*`. A catch-all would also swallow `/chat/999` and every
# other unmatched nested path, answering "no profile called that" to a question
# nobody asked. Assets are matched before this runs, so real routes are
# unaffected; a single unknown segment is a handle now, and the screen says so
# when nobody holds it.
#
# `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` is two segments and cannot match —
# which matters, because both platforms follow zero redirects when verifying it.
/:username  /[username].html  200
